# 6. Dynamic Expert Spawning and Bottom-Up Routing

## 6.1 The Failure of Fixed-Capacity and Flat Architectures
Flat MARL architectures collapse at the Sparsity Wall due to temporal discount dilution ($ \gamma^{250} \approx 0.08 $). Causal Affordance Routing (CAR) and Causal Advantage Routing (CIR) fail. Monolithic architectures lack temporal abstraction and modular capacity. Traditional H-MARL approaches like ROMA or MAHIRO introduce this abstraction. They rely on fixed-capacity networks or top-down manager-worker dichotomies. They are prone to non-stationarity and goal collapse.

We introduce a decentralized paradigm. It is a Dynamic Skill-Pool Co-op architecture. We refer to it as CO-OP (Confidence-Oriented Option Pool).

## 6.2 The CO-OP Architecture

CO-OP decouples the physical agent from the policy. The architecture consists of a dynamically expanding pool of Expert Neural Networks $ \mathcal{E} = \{E_1, E_2, \dots, E_K\} $.

Any agent $ i $ can route its execution to any Expert in the pool. The agent's identity vector $ e_{\text{role}_i} $ is concatenated with the local observation $ o_i $ before being processed by the Expert.

### 6.2.1 Bottom-Up Confidence Routing
CO-OP employs a bottom-up voting mechanism. It replaces a top-down manager. 

At step $ t $, the state $ s_t $ is passed to all $ K $ currently active Experts. Each Expert $ E_k $ outputs an action distribution $ \pi_k(a|s_t) $ and a Value prediction $ V_k(s_t) $. The Value prediction serves as the Expert's Confidence Score. The global router selects the Expert with the highest confidence:
$$$
k^* = \arg\max_{k \in \{1 \dots K\}} V_k(s_t)
$$$
The agent executes the action sampled from $ \pi_{k^*} $. Causal Affordance Credit (CAR) is routed to the winning Expert. 

CO-OP eliminates manual reward shaping by implementing structural affordance detection. It detects an affordance by evaluating the team's aggregate action-mask volume. If $ \sum \text{mask}_{t+1} > \sum \text{mask}_t $ and an agent executed the `INTERACT` action, that Expert is causally credited for expanding the team's physical capabilities.

### 6.2.2 Asymmetric Failure Shielding Integration
CO-OP integrates MARC's Asymmetric Failure Shielding and Macro Weighting. 

The base rewards for the active experts are modulated by a macro weighting factor $ \Omega_t $:
$$$
\Omega_t = \begin{cases} 
\exp\left(-\alpha \frac{A_t}{A_{max}}\right) & \text{if Expert } k \text{ triggered an affordance} \\
O \cdot \exp\left(-\alpha \frac{A_t}{A_{max}}\right) & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
$$$
Here, $ O \in \{1.0, -0.5\} $ is the binary terminal outcome of the episode, and $ A_t $ is the global alarm level, normalized by $ A_{max} $. CO-OP multiplies the immediate rewards by $ \Omega_t $ before calculating the Generalized Advantage Estimate. It shields competent upstream experts from downstream incompetence. The decentralized router accurately assesses confidence.

### 6.2.3 Dynamic Capacity Evolution (Spawning and Pruning)
CO-OP dynamically adjusts the size of the Expert pool $ K $. 

If an agent encounters a novel state space and the maximum confidence across all experts drops below a critical threshold $ \tau_{spawn} $:
$$$
\max(V_1(s_t), \dots, V_K(s_t)) < \tau_{spawn}
$$$
A new Expert $ E_{K+1} $ is appended to the pool. Neural network weights initialize to output near $ 0.0 $. Spawning strictly requires all other experts to fall below $ \tau_{spawn} $. The newly spawned $ E_{K+1} $ natively wins the vote. This is optimistic nativism. It bypasses Upper Confidence Bound algorithms. New experts are selected to explore unknown states.

A global complexity penalty $ -\lambda \cdot K $ is applied to the reward stream. If an Expert $ E_k $ is not selected by any agent for a continuous window of $ W $ episodes, it is pruned from the pool.
